a history walk gives the draft back

§6: *"composer draft preserved across a history walk"*.

Pressing Up to glance at the previous message and Down to come back set the buffer to
**the empty string**. A half-written prompt was gone, and it was the quietest kind of
loss: nothing was sent anywhere, nothing was recorded, and there is no undo entry —
a recall is navigation, not an edit, so `undo` never saw it. Every shell on the box
keeps the draft, which is also why nobody would think to check.

## The fix, and the one place the walk is known to have begun

`Editor` gains `draft: Option<String>` — *the buffer as it was when the walk started* —
saved on the one transition that begins a walk (`history_at` is `None` and the key is
Up) and **taken**, not read, when the walk ends. Taking it is the part that matters: a
second walk then saves its own draft instead of inheriting the first one's, and the test
asserts exactly that with a fresh line rather than by reasoning about it.

Checked in `recall` rather than in `vertical`, so the two entry points into a walk (the
arrow keys and the unwired Up/Down arm) cannot disagree about when a walk starts.

`take` also clears the draft, because a submitted line is not a draft.

## The rule that stayed, and my test was wrong about it

The walk refuses to move once a recalled entry has been **edited** — *"the next press
would silently destroy the edit"* — and that guard now also covers the restored draft:
after coming back to it, the draft is an edited recall, so another Up is refused rather
than overwriting what was typed.

**My first version of the test asserted a second walk at that point and it failed**, and
the failure is worth a line in this message because it is the useful kind: the assertion
was wrong, not the guard. The test now asserts the refusal and then, separately, that a
walk on a *fresh* line keeps its own draft — which is what the `take` is for.

## Test

`a_history_walk_restores_the_draft_it_interrupted` — a two-entry history, a typed draft,
Up twice to the oldest entry, Down twice back to the draft; then the refusal after an
edit to it; then a fresh line whose walk keeps its own draft; then a one-entry history
where the walk never leaves the newest entry and still comes back.

`cargo test -p letibot-ui`: **103 passed, 0 failed** (102 + 1).
`cargo test -p letibot-tui`: **312 passed, 0 failed, 4 ignored**.

**Not verified, and said rather than glossed:** `rustfmt --check` cannot run — it writes
a temp file and `bash` is closed for that direction by the circuit breaker, which only
the operator lifts. The four crate suites outside these two are still unrun since
`65725ce`, for the lock reasons the last five commits record.

## Not in this commit

The rest of §6's list, one item per commit: `/todos`, `/subagents`, `/peek`, `/resume`,
`/promote`, `q` closing any pane, `ctrl-o` into a subagent, clicks reaching five panes
instead of two, SGR mouse motion decoded, and the tail-origin scroll sign for the peek
and job-output panes.
